erial on  to you know for purposes of the job  really  once you become aware that this  potential for exposure is there  you at least have the ability to start  minimizing that and making sure that  you're  only using it when you absolutely need  to  so rob finally let me ask you has the  industry accepted any of the findings  are they making any  uh changes in their production in the  way they manufacture these chemicals or  is there another way to waterproof this  gear  yeah what we've seen is as information  finally started to come out to the  public to the scientific world to the  regulatory world over the last  uh oh gosh man several years as this  information finally came out about the  toxicity of pfoa  and related chemical like pfos that are  part of this forever chemical  phos family what we saw happen was the  manufacturers  of those pfoa pfos the c8s  that are the the ones of concern that  have been used in these  fabric treatments in many years past  manufacturers started switching away  and started moving to different  chemicals  but unfortunately some of the chemicals  they moved to  were just slightly different as you  heard dr peaslee mentioned  instead of a c8 maybe they knocked a  couple of carbons off and called it c6  or c4s  and unfortunately that stuff is now  being put out into the market  being used in these products and the  scientific world  is still investigating to what extent  these chemicals really  are all that much safer you know will  they in fact  not build up in our blood to the same  degree that the other ones do  and unfortunately some of the toxicology  data that's coming in on  animal studies that are starting to be  done are showing some of the same toxic  effects  of the old chemicals so there's this  real concern as you heard about  regrettable substitutions  you know are we simply moving from one  problem to another  when there are a lot of folks that are  out saying we ought to be moving away  from this whole  pfas chemistry into alternative  chemicals  for coatings of these types of materials  so there are a lot of folks looking into  this now  and one of the things i'm really trying  to do is make sure that the science  is done by independent scientists to  confirm  what this big group and class of  chemicals are actually doing to us  the companies shouldn't be allowed to  sit back say  you can't prove that this is causing  harm yet not do the studies that are  necessary to tell people  to tell us exactly what these chemicals  are doing to us  when they all get in us in mix and get  into our blood  that's something i'm trying to pursue  right now through a lawsuit we filed a  couple years ago and  actually our lead plaintiff is an ohio  firefighter  where we're trying to not get money but  get  studies in confirmations conformational  studies done  of what these chemicals can do to people  who are exposed including firefighters  who might be exposed through  their equipment or through the  firefighting foams  all right rob a lot thank you so much we  look forward to seeing more about the  studies  and the continuing case and cause for  concern thanks so very much  coming up next this week's winners and  losers in the media  covering the insurrection in washington  see if you agree with the editors of  mediaite  you're watching brian ross investigates  on the law and crime trial network  time now for this week's winners and  losers in the media  as chosen by the editors of media we're  joined by aidan mclaughlin who is the  editor-in-chief of mediaite  which like the law and crime trial  network is part of the dan abrams media  empire  and aiden the big winners were at cnn  with a  shakeup of assignments what happened  that's right so both cnn and fox news  announced their new  programming slates uh for the new  administration uh this typically happens  you know around when we have a  transition of power  from one president to another uh the big  win  at cnn uh in their shake up was jake  tapper he's the anchor of uh the day  show  the lead um which is on at 4pm and uh he  got given an extra hour  uh daily uh in the 5 p.m hour shortening  wolf blitzer  who was uh on after him on cnn um so now  jake tapper's hosting  two hours a day during the weekdays uh  which is a serious vote of confidence  from  uh the bosses at cnn uh and reflects the  the role that jake tapper has taken at  the network  as sort of the washington uh anchor that  covers all the big news events in  washington  he certainly has become the lead anchor  and of course in uh television like that  air time is everything  so who were the winners or losers at fox  right so fox news had their own uh  shake-up of their programming  um theirs was really remarkable they  basically put a new opinion hour  uh at 7 00 p.m on the network which is  sort of just before their big opinion  primetime slate uh they haven't  announced a  a host for that hour of television um  but the  the person who occupied that hour was  martha mccallum who actually had a news  show  the story at 7 pm now to make way for  that new opinion show  martha mccallum has been knocked back to  3 p.m uh which was shepard smith's old  spot  now fox's is adamant that this is not an  emotion for martha mccallum they're just  saying that you know 3 p.m is a news  hour and 7 p.m was an opinion one  so they're just changing her around a  little bit but it's certainly a less  less coveted real estate uh at fox news  um that also means that bill hemmer who  had 3pm and replaced  shepard smith has been moved back to  mornings he's now anchoring  from 9 to 11 at fox news  all right and they all scramble to  figure out how to cover the new  president president biden  to be sworn in next week aidan  mclaughlin thank you so much for joining  us and thanks to all of you for watching  and thanks to our great team along crime  for getting us on the air  and off the air have a good evening  [Music]  you
 

